WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.080
LEE BOONSTRA: Hi, everybody.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.810
My name is Lee Boonstra.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.940
I work as a customer
engineer for Google

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.860
from the Amsterdam office.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.700
And you will see me mostly
visiting banks and insurances,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.680
as we, at Google, defined
our teams in pods.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.070
And they asked
me, Lee, would you

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.230
like to go to Paris to give this
presentation about extending

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.730
G Suite functionalities
with business applications?

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.439
And I said, yeah, I'm sure.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:33.640
I'm a developer.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.050
I love to share my knowledge
about building cool apps.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.440
So yes, I would
like to go to Paris.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.980
Are there any developers
here, in the room?

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.920
A few.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.980
How many people have ever
seen code or read code?

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.570
Some more, right?

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.251
So this will be a
technical session,

00:00:57.251 --> 00:00:59.500
but I'm going to share with
you all the secrets on how

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.025
you can build applications
with the use of G Suite.

00:01:03.025 --> 00:01:05.019
And typically,
there are two ways

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:07.690
in how you can build
applications with G Suite.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.240
You can take that
Apps Script approach--

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.380
that means I'm staying
within the G Suite domain

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.970
and I put my own
code on top of that.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.590
So it is within G Suite.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.600
Let's say, in Docs or Sheets.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.750
And I put my own code
within the doc or sheet.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.060
And that way, I make
it more advanced

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.640
or more conformed to my needs.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.910
Back in the past, people used
to use, for example, in Office,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.450
they used Macros and
Visual Basic that way.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.570
We, in G Suite,
have Apps Script.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.900
So that is one approach.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.080
The other approach
is I already have

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.770
my own existing application,
or my own app, or website,

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:50.140
and I like to take G
Suite functionalities

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.120
within my own app.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.127
So the benefit is then
I'm not, internally,

00:01:55.127 --> 00:01:56.210
within the G Suite domain.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.180
Now I can be on any domain.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.270
And I just move the data from
G Suite or the functionalities

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.900
from G Suite within the app.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.810
Now there's actually another
way in how you can create

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.190
applications with G Suite.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.770
And maybe you have seen this,
earlier today, in the keynote.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.025
That is through App Maker.

00:02:13.025 --> 00:02:14.400
And I will show
you that as well.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.680
App Maker is our visual
design, drag-and-drop editor,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.930
where you very quickly
can create apps.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.650
You don't need to be a developer
to create applications.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.550
For the people
that earlier said,

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.200
I've never seen code before--

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.950
yeah, you guys can
create apps as well.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.190
After this all, I will hand
over to Laura from LumApps.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.796
And she will explain what
they did with their company--

00:02:35.796 --> 00:02:37.420
what kind of applications
they created.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.920
So very exciting.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.120
All right.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.970
Let's start with the first
approach-- the Apps Script

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.550
approach.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:53.947
Apps Script is a serverless
JavaScript runtime for G Suite.

00:02:53.947 --> 00:02:55.530
Serverless-- that
means not that there

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.100
are no servers, because there
are servers, but they are

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.910
are somewhere in a
Google data center

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.110
and we just don't
care about that.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.630
But we write JavaScript code.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.720
And it's been executed
somewhere in a data center.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.750
And you can do this to
create the G Suite add ons.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.850
Now these add ons
for G Suite, they

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.520
make use of the OAuth
2 model, so they

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.950
are secure within
the G Suite domain.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.860
There is a script editor within
G Suite that you can use,

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.680
where you can start
coding your code.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.660
And you can make
use of triggers.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.760
So for example, when
you open a document,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.640
it starts executing code.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.550
Now these add ons--

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.519
you can bring them
to a Marketplace

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:39.810
and then you can download them.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.150
You can also download them
from the document itself.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.040
Then you go to Add
Ons, Get Add Ons,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.485
and download these add ons
to make it more advanced.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.540
Now let's look into
the entry points--

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.170
how can I get
started with G Suite?

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.800
The first entry point is
like container bound apps.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.510
Now what is a
container bound app?

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:05.050
That means I have one
particular Google Doc or one

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.950
particular sheet, and every
time, when I open that sheet,

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:13.260
I execute my own code,
my own Apps Script code.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.180
That is a container
bound application.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.647
So it's for one
particular document.

00:04:19.647 --> 00:04:21.730
And you can get access to
that particular document

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:24.440
by running
DocumentApp.getActiveDocument,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:25.940
SpreadsheetApp.g
etActiveSpreadheet,

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.260
or FormApp.getActiveForm.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:32.680
And it has these triggers,
so every time you

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.830
open the document,
you can execute code,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.370
or every time you edit the
document, it executes code.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.560
And the other approach that is
not for a particular document,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.850
that can be any document
or any spreadsheet--

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.750
you go to scripts.google.com.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.270
And you get the editor.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.670
But your code that you
will execute in this case

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.914
can be for any document.

00:04:55.914 --> 00:04:57.330
So usually, what
people do is they

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.380
create a picker or
a dropdown where

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.390
you can select the
document where you want

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.850
to run your code on top of.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.640
Or you create a new
document or a new sheet,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.040
and you run code on
that type of sheet.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.440
This typically done for add ons.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.690
Like when you download an
add on from the Marketplace,

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.950
they probably chose this way.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:23.610
So not a container bound script,
it's just a regular Apps Script

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.550
application.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.800
There is another way in
how you can create code

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.680
in Apps Script that
is for AdWords.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.190
We're not going to cover that.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.200
And the last way is
through App Maker.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.440
Although it is a visual
design, drag-and-drop editor,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.840
it is still possible
to add some code,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.390
in case you want to create a
really advanced application.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.810
We'll see this later.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.830
Now how can you write
Apps Script code?

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:49.330
Let's take a look
into an example

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:53.470
of how I can create or
modify just standard G Suite

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:57.130
functionality with my own code.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:00.100
Usually what I do is I go
and look into the docs.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.200
In the docs, they
give great guides

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.050
and they explain the whole
APIs that you can start using.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:08.990
So I always have that open in
another tab, in the browser.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.170
And then I finally
start writing code.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.510
So in this case, what
I'm kind of built

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:19.360
is a conference app, where I
have a shared slides folder.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.630
And my coworkers, they can
upload a slide in my folder.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:26.170
And every time people
upload slides in the folder,

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:27.550
I get an email.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.420
It's a very simple example,
but I think it's a use case--

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.930
getting an email based on when a
file has being edited, I think,

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.332
is a very common use case.

00:06:37.332 --> 00:06:38.290
So we start doing that.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:44.140
We go into Drive, where we
create a new Apps Script file.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.110
That is the file that
we will start to use.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.200
The next thing is we need this
slides folder, where people

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.200
will upload the files in.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.120
And to do that, we need to
make sure that it's shared.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.440
So within your drive,
you can say, OK,

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:00.580
this is a shared folder.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:01.770
You right click it.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.420
You set link sharing on.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.160
And you usually get a pop up.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.700
And it also tells you the ID of
the folder that will be shared.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:12.370
And that is the ID that we
need to reference in our code,

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:14.680
so that our code knows,
OK, this is the folder

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:18.424
that we need to
start monitoring.

00:07:18.424 --> 00:07:19.090
So there you go.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:23.570
I start writing a variable
with the ID of the folder.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.170
Now the next thing is I start
writing some logics here.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:29.320
And the first thing that
I'm doing here is I say,

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:35.710
OK, I'll take that folder and
I'll get a real folder object.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:37.810
And from that folder
object, I want

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.540
to loop through all the files
that are in this folder.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.020
And I save that to a variable.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.326
This will be an array.

00:07:43.326 --> 00:07:44.950
So later, I can look
through that array

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:47.050
and start doing things.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.220
I create another
variable called Props.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.390
And Props, in this case,
is like a very little store

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.136
where you can store data.

00:07:55.136 --> 00:07:56.510
And it's like a
persistent layer.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:08:00.490
So it's very simple, but it's
a way you can save files.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.000
Even after you
close the document,

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.721
you still can store
some information there.

00:08:06.721 --> 00:08:08.470
And we need to store
information because I

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:12.412
need to mark if I have seen this
slide before or not, because I

00:08:12.412 --> 00:08:14.620
don't want to have, by the
end of the month, hundreds

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.190
e-mails because it started
e-mailing out the same files

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:21.040
every time.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.000
All right.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.260
I create a variable,
New Files Array.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:27.610
So here, I will flag it--
like, OK, this file is new.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:30.040
And then I start looping
through all the files

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.960
that are in the
shared slides folder.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:34.580
And here comes the trick--

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:39.740
I say, OK, if I have
nothing in my Props store--

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:42.710
so if I have nothing
stored or saved yet--

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:46.250
then I'm going to create
a key in this store that

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.950
references the file that
I am viewing right now.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.700
And the reason why I'll
do that is so next time,

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:54.110
I will skip this.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:55.670
But if you don't
skip it, then it

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:57.770
adds it to the New Files Array.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.117
And when it comes to
the New Files Array,

00:09:00.117 --> 00:09:01.700
then it's the last
thing that it does.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.470
It's like, OK,
there are new files,

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:08.540
new slides have been uploaded,
I need to email these slides.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:10.590
And that's what
the Gmail app does.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.750
This is Google Gmail API.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.530
There's a standard integrated
within Google Apps Script.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.470
You can start making
use of these API calls.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.440
You don't need to
download anything or start

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:26.560
authenticating anything because
it's all standard there.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:30.312
So within 20 lines, I
created this functionality.

00:09:30.312 --> 00:09:31.895
I can start playing
the functionality.

00:09:31.895 --> 00:09:33.350
I run the Play button.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.850
I say execute the
process function.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:40.070
And I'm good to go, right?

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:42.380
I make sure that I
have logging enabled,

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:45.200
so every time when
something will be emailed,

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.510
I will also get a log message.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:48.890
I can do simple logging--

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:50.720
that is just with logger.log.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:52.710
But I can also do
more advanced logging.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.470
And that goes through the
Google Cloud Stackdriver.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:58.820
So if you really want to
have row numbers and so,

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:02.814
then the Stackdriver
is much, much nicer.

00:10:02.814 --> 00:10:04.480
There, you see this
is the email and how

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.890
it will look like in my Gmail
when I get the slides being

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:09.922
e-mailed to me.

00:10:09.922 --> 00:10:11.380
Now there is one
thing that I did--

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.440
I executed the
process function once,

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.146
just for testing purposes.

00:10:16.146 --> 00:10:17.770
But what I need to
do is I need to have

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:20.560
this run in the background,
so that every time when

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.260
somebody uploads
slides, I get the email.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.870
So to do that,
you need triggers.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:28.210
I'm going to set up
a project trigger.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.160
And you see, this is my
first code that I created,

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.250
so there is no
trigger created yet.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.760
So I click on that link.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.910
And then it says, yeah, you
need to authenticate first.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:41.240
We need authorization.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:42.020
This is secure.

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:47.840
We don't want everybody to
get access to these Gmail APIs

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.720
without giving the right
permissions for it.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:55.540
It's OAuth 2, so we need
to review the permissions.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.360
Usually, this is
all out of the box.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:58.420
It happens for you.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:10:59.860
So you get this pop up.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:02.950
And then I say,
OK, I am Lee and I

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.650
like to retrieve e-mails
through my Gmail,

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.410
so I authorize
Gmail, in this case.

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:11.260
And I also give you access
to my slides folder,

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.150
which is in Drive.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.100
So I allow that.

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:16.920
Now I can finally
set up the triggers.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.390
And what I'm doing
here is I create

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.880
a time driven trigger that
runs in the background,

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:22.500
every five minutes.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:23.980
And then I get the email.

00:11:27.027 --> 00:11:28.860
In case you think, oh,
this Apps Script, I'd

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.170
like to try it out
with that, go ahead.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.960
There are actually some
extras coming soon.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.700
We will have support
for Gmail add ons.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.520
So if you want to
extend on top of Gmail,

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.440
that is possible to Apps Script.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.690
We will have support
for Slides, so that you

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.072
can extend on top of Slides.

00:11:45.072 --> 00:11:46.530
If you're a heavy
Slides user, that

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:49.380
might be nice that you can
put your own functionalities

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.310
on top of that.

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:53.220
If you try that out, there is an
early access program for this.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.530
So you can go to this URL,
and then you can sign up,

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:57.900
and you can start
playing around with that.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.900
And also, what's coming soon--
those are mobile add ons.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:05.440
So the add ons also
run on a mobile phone.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.170
That's pretty cool.

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.530
Now let's take a look into
the second approach, where

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.180
I said that this is
the approach where

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.180
you have your own application
and you want to integrate

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:20.340
Google data, or G Suite data,
or G Suite functionalities

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:21.660
within your own app--

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:25.170
so your own app, on your
own website, for example.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.540
You can do that through
the Google APIs.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.960
Google has lots of
clients' SDKs for you.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:35.160
So whether you're a Java
developer, or a PHP developer,

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:40.020
or Go, or Python, or, like
me, JavaScript and Node.js.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:44.130
You can download all these
SDKs from the website.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:44.910
They're all there.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:52.000
And such an SDK, they contain
all the Google endpoints.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.320
So for all Google products,
there are SDKs available.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.540
So if you think, I
like Google Maps,

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:01.530
you can extend your own
applications with Google Maps,

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.950
or you can extend your own
applications with Gmail,

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.670
or Google Docs functionalities.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:12.250
This is all integrated
within these client SDKs.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:16.660
So let's look into an example
of how you would do this.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:19.030
Like I said already, I
am a Node.js developer,

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:20.240
so I would prefer nodes.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:23.260
So what I do is I go to the
node package manager page

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:26.380
and I start downloading
the Google API.

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:29.170
And then the next thing
that you need to do

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:34.210
is you need to go to the
Google Cloud control panel.

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:36.370
And then you look for
that particular API

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.060
that you want to make use of.

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:41.290
So in my case, I'm going to
create a Gmail application,

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:45.970
so I need to enable the Gmail
API within my Cloud console.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:47.980
This way, I can
monitor and I can also

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:53.770
see how many times people
have been using my Gmail APIs.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.980
So you enable that
in the console.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:59.390
The next thing is you need
to set up credentials.

00:13:59.390 --> 00:14:01.960
This is a very similar
process as if you would

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:04.780
create applications in GCP.

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:06.760
So you create a
service account, you

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:10.360
can download an adjacent
key on your machine.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:17.200
And for the Gmail API and for,
probably, all the other APIs,

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.210
then you start writing code.

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:21.340
So where in Apps
Script it went out

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.950
of the box-- the OAuth
functionality-- here,

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.100
we need to trigger
that with code.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:31.510
And the way how OAuth 2
works, in general, is you

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:33.760
have this key.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:37.840
And with that key, you will
log into your Google account.

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.330
So you get just a Google pop up.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:42.910
You get a Google pop up when
you go to a particular URL.

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:44.710
So, usually, in
your application,

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:50.170
you create a button that says
Set Up This Functionality.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:53.200
You might remember
that from applications

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.330
on your mobile phone
that make use of Google.

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.250
And, usually, it
opens a Google page

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:59.080
in the background
with a Google log in.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:01.236
You need to log into
your Google account.

00:15:01.236 --> 00:15:02.860
And what happens in
the background is--

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:05.810
this is after you've logged
into that Google account--

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.320
they will redirect
you to another page.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:10.870
But they have this
token being generated.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:13.810
And that token will be
written down on your device

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:16.330
or it will be written
down on your machine.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:21.190
And that is a bearer token that,
later on, will give you access

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:24.400
to your Gmail API or
to your Google APIs.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.070
And you usually set it
up with an expiry date.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:30.700
So for a certain frame, you
can get access to that API

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.070
without going through this
process and by logging

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:34.190
into Google every time.

00:15:37.154 --> 00:15:38.570
Here, you can see
an example code.

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:41.000
Now this is already a
little bit more trickier,

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.190
but, honestly, it
wasn't that difficult

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.520
because I just copied it
from the Google Docs site.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:47.780
They have this example
of an OAuth flow,

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:51.140
and I could just copy,
paste it, and it works.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:58.662
Basically, it says I used
the Node.js Google API.

00:15:58.662 --> 00:16:00.680
I need to define the scope.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:02.690
So in this case, I'm
making use of Gmail.

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.440
So I manually need to say
this is the Gmail scope.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:08.240
Where in Apps Script,
it automatically

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:09.860
showed up in a pop
up. here, I need

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.920
to type it and tell
Google, this is the scope

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.150
that I want to use.

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:17.750
I also need to specify the
path where, on my machine,

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.460
the token will be stored.

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.690
And then I just copy
it over the methods

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:24.890
that will take care
of the authorization.

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:28.180
And it will work out of the box.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:31.595
And then you can start
using Gmail code.

00:16:31.595 --> 00:16:33.970
And Gmail code-- that could
look like this-- for example,

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:37.480
you say,
gmail.users.messages.list.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:39.670
I specify my authentication.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:42.910
That is the object that
was saved on my machine.

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:46.990
I tell who is the user ID that
I want to read to Gmail logics

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:47.720
from.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:49.480
Well, in this case, that's me.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:50.830
And what are my querying?

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:54.250
I'm querying, I want to
read all the unread email.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:56.260
So I want to get that.

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:57.820
And I get that here.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:16:59.660
If it went wrong,
it will log it.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:03.380
But if it's OK, I will save
it to the e-mails variable.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:07.390
And in this case, it
contains email IDs.

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:09.910
And I could look through
the arrays with IDs,

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:15.790
and get the full email bodies,
and you can display an email.

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:18.040
I used this code, actually,
for playing around

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:19.300
with my Google Home.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.310
Google Home can
read up my e-mails.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:22.310
So I did it this way.

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.770
Now if you want to make
use of this kind of stuff,

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:29.410
there are API for G Suite--

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:30.880
for every G Suite product.

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:35.620
So I mentioned Gmail, but there
are APIs for Contacts, APIs

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:40.040
for Sheets, for Sites, for
you name it-- everything.

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:49.960
We have seen App Maker and
we have seen Google APIs.

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:53.170
Now let's take a look
into our App Maker.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.690
App Maker-- this is our
visual design editor.

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:57.250
So you don't need
to be a developer

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.320
to create Google applications.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:01.870
You can very quickly
drag and drop it.

00:18:01.870 --> 00:18:03.400
It's a SaaS application.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:07.506
That means that you go to
App Maker in your browser,

00:18:07.506 --> 00:18:09.130
and it opens it in
the browser, and you

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:13.030
can start making use of it.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:14.920
It has a bunch of benefits.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:17.650
I mean, obviously,
it's a very fast way

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:19.810
to create great
applications this way

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.050
because you drag and drop
components on the stage.

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:25.420
But it also has
data binding, which

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:29.060
means that if I create
data in my application--

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.780
and then you find a model.

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:33.580
Let's say a model
would be a car.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:37.740
Let's say I'm going to collect
car information in my app.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.940
And if you would
define a car model,

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:41.860
you would write
out the properties

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:45.730
of a car, which is number of
wheels, the color, the brand.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:49.930
So you define the structure
of your objects in a model.

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.780
And because of
automatically data binding,

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:54.890
it recognizes that data.

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:58.510
So if you drag View
Components on the stage,

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.930
like a table, or a
grid, or a chart,

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:06.190
it will automatically recognize
the models that belong to it

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:07.330
and automatically show it.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:11.190
You don't need to code
anything for that.

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:14.170
It's nicely integrated
within the G Suite domain.

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:17.410
You can make use of a Cloud SQL.

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.900
So the GCP Cloud SQL-- you
can implement it in your apps.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.840
But if you don't
have GCP Cloud SQL

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:25.900
and you just want to make
use of G Suite on its own,

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:29.860
you can also just store
the data in Google Drive.

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:32.170
And it's secure because it's
within the Google domain,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:36.430
so all the security rules
are applied and have

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:40.300
been whitelistd to
do G Suite, itself.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:44.080
So this is not a solution
for creating apps

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:49.070
for the Google Play Store
or the iOS App Store.

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:49.570
No.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:53.510
This is more a solution
for internal applications,

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:57.730
like an internal HR
application, or an internal CRM,

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:00.010
or tracking applications.

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:02.500
There are lots of
reasons why businesses

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:08.110
need internal apps that run
within their own firewall.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:10.030
And this time, they
don't need to hire

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:11.480
developers to build this stuff.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:11.980
No.

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:13.300
They can do it themselves.

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:15.970
They can prototype it
quickly or they could even

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:19.690
build full life applications.

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:22.660
So you don't need to be a
developer to make use of it.

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:24.850
It is point and click,
so you can drag and drop

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:26.470
all the components.

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:28.510
It's supported by
open standards,

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:30.830
so it makes use of HTML5.

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:33.370
So JavaScript use HTML.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:36.760
And it also has the nice
material design look and feel,

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:40.030
so your applications really
look like Android apps.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.750
Really nice.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:45.030
Now you have me here,
talking about this.

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:49.750
Now let me give you a demo, so
I can show you how this works.

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:51.620
So can we switch to
my machine, please?

00:20:54.819 --> 00:20:56.650
Cool.

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:59.770
So what you see here--

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:02.800
this is the SaaS
application of App Maker.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:05.170
And I recorded it
myself, so I'll

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:09.231
make sure that I can do
it within the time frame.

00:21:09.231 --> 00:21:10.730
The first thing
that I am doing here

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:14.355
is I am changing the
application name.

00:21:14.355 --> 00:21:16.510
Now what kind of
application are we creating?

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:18.340
We're creating a
to-do application.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:22.480
I think that's the
most common application

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:25.310
for when you're new.

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.030
So I create this application.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:31.740
Now I'm going to select a
data model or a data object.

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:34.820
And, like I said, you
can save data in Drive

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:39.380
or you can save data in
an in Google Cloud SQL.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.830
I'm going to create a save
data in the Google Drive.

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:46.640
So I select a model
in the Google Drive.

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:47.330
Let's do that.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:54.640
There are other ways as well,
but we're using just Drive.

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:58.540
So I select Drive
and click Next.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:00.320
Now I give a model name.

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:02.500
So in this case, the
